# Differential test of loop-fresh-arrays.js: an empty array literal inside a
# loop is a new array each iteration, so the C++ declares its vector inside
# the loop body, not in a slot at the function's entry (EmitC/Vectors.cpp,
# replaceStringArray). The C++ is compiled against public Core, checked for
# interpreter symbols with nm, run, and every global compared with the
# interpreter's (compilation-unit.py). The negative prefixes the printed
# String `a` (--mutate-as string) and requires the comparison to fail naming
# it. string-arrays-calls.py is the generic pipeline driver for a fixture
# that is not registered in NativeFixtures.cmake.

# RUN: python3 %S/string-arrays-calls.py --translate ctjs-translate --opt ctjs-opt --js %S/loop-fresh-arrays.js --out %t/loop-fresh-arrays.emitc.mlir
# RUN: ctjs-translate --mlir-to-cpp %t/loop-fresh-arrays.emitc.mlir | FileCheck %s
# RUN: %compilation_unit --module %t/loop-fresh-arrays.emitc.mlir --js %S/loop-fresh-arrays.js --work %t --name loop_fresh_arrays --core-build %build
# RUN: not %compilation_unit --module %t/loop-fresh-arrays.emitc.mlir --js %S/loop-fresh-arrays.js --work %t --name loop_fresh_arrays_negative --core-build %build --mutate a --mutate-as string 2>&1 | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=NEGATIVE

# CHECK: do {
# CHECK: if (
# CHECK-NEXT: std::vector<ctnative::js_string> [[PAIRS:v[0-9]+]];
# CHECK: ctnative::Array.prototype.push.call([[PAIRS]],
# NEGATIVE: global 'a' differs
